==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|rack one's brains}}
* {{l|en|wrack one's brain}}
* {{l|en|wrack one's brains}}

===Etymology===
Combination of {{m|en|rack||medieval torture method}}, and {{m|en|brain}}, thus meaning “to cause the mind extreme discomfort”.

Variants include {{m|en|rack one's wit}} (1583) and {{m|en|rack one's memory}},<ref name="quinion">{{cite-web
|title=Wrack or rack?
|url=http://www.worldwidewords.org/qa/qa-wra1.htm
|author=Michael Quinion
|date=2011-11-19
|site=World Wide Words
}}</ref> as well as {{m|en|cudgel one's brain}} / {{m|en|cudgel one's brains}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-rack one's brain.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{head|en|verb|head=[[rack]] one's [[brain]]}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[struggle]] to [[think]] of or [[remember]] something.
#: ''I have been '''racking my brain''' all day trying to think of a birthday present for her.''

